---
/**
 * The fleet and the four opponents, drawn once.
 *
 * ⚠️ **A file of its own because two components need the same drawings**: the
 * game itself, and the thumbnail on the games index, which is a real board at a
 * card's size rather than a picture of one. Copying a hundred lines of hull
 * into the second of them is exactly how a card ends up advertising a game that
 * no longer looks like that.
 *
 * ⚠️ **The ids are global, so this must appear once per document.** It never
 * appears twice as things stand - the board is only on the game page and the
 * thumbnail only on the index - and if that ever changes, this is the note that
 * says why the second one is a duplicate id rather than a second sprite sheet.
 */
---

{/*
  The fleet: five vessels, and five silhouettes of them.

  ⚠️ **Two symbols per ship, and the pair is the whole trick.** A hull is drawn
  eight times over, stacked up the Z axis, to give it a side you can see on a
  tilted board - and only the top copy is ever looked at. So `bs-ship-N` carries
  the turrets, the funnels, the flight deck and the dive planes, and
  `bs-hull-N` is the outline alone, which is what the seven copies underneath
  use. Detail is resolved once per ship instead of eight times.

  ⚠️ **Every vessel is a plan view, bow to the right, lit from the top.** The
  rounding is bands rather than gradients: a `<linearGradient>` inside a symbol
  is not reliably resolved from inside a `<use>` shadow tree, while a flat fill
  mixed off `--bs-hull` always is. Four bands - a lit sheer, the flank, the
  shaded flank, and the boot topping at the waterline - read as a round hull and
  cost nothing to resolve.

  ⚠️ **Colour goes in through `style`, never through a `fill` attribute.** These
  are `<use>`d, and a document stylesheet cannot reach inside that shadow tree -
  inherited custom properties are the only thing that gets in. That is also what
  makes a wreck one rule: `.bs-hull.is-sunk` redefines the tokens and every
  shape in here follows, down to the red at the waterline.

  A vertical ship is the same drawing turned a quarter by the stylesheet, which
  is why nothing in here has a top or a bottom that only works one way up.
*/}
